    8969	### §C1 — Odlyzko zeros at height 10²² (CLOSED 2026-04-26, S71, mode I)
    8970	
    8971	**Outcome:** structural negative result + new quantitative obstruction.
    8972	
    8973	Re-ran the S49 BK arithmetic-correction probe on Odlyzko's published
    8974	tables `zeros4` (n ∈ [10²¹+1, 10²¹+10⁴], height T ≈ 1.4·10²⁰, L = 44.6)
    8975	and `zeros5` (n ∈ [10²²+1, 10²²+10⁴], T ≈ 1.4·10²¹, L = 46.8). Used a
    8976	**random-prime null** (template with primes replaced by uniform
    8977	pseudo-primes in [2, 50]) as the proper test instead of the biased
    8978	gap-shuffled null. Empirical Pearson with the BK template is
    8979	statistically indistinguishable from the random-prime null
    8980	(z = -0.94σ, +0.93σ); prime-frequency Fourier amplitudes are NOT
    8981	enhanced over random frequencies (ratios 0.95, 0.99).
    8982	
    8983	Why §C1 fails by structure: the BK signal scales as `13.6/L²` while
    8984	empirical noise scales as `4/√N`. Detection requires
    8985	**N ≥ 0.09 κ² L⁴** for κ-σ Pearson detection. At Odlyzko's heights
    8986	this needs N ≈ 3·10⁵-4·10⁵ zeros; Odlyzko publishes 10⁴, short by
    8987	~35×. Pushing to higher heights makes the situation *worse* because
    8988	L⁴ grows faster than any reasonable N. The asymptotic regime
    8989	*suppresses* the BK signal faster than data accumulation can
    8990	compensate.
    8991	
    8992	This sharpens E7.1 / E1.10 / E3.13 from qualitative "BK undetected"
    8993	to quantitative "BK detection requires N ≥ 0.81 L⁴ zeros" — a hard
    8994	scaling barrier independent of computational budget.
