    1711	`max(|A+A|, |A·A|) ≥ |A|^{15/14} / (log|A|)^{2/7}` for any `A` with
    1712	`|A| ≤ p^{7/13}(log p)^{-4/13}`. The bound is for ARBITRARY `A`. The
    1713	question: is the *empirical* sum-product gain
    1714	`g(A) := max(|A+A|, |A·A|) / |A|^{15/14}` strictly LARGER for the prime
    1715	set than for matched-cardinality random subsets of `F_p`? If yes,
    1716	primes carry an additive-multiplicative joint structure invisible to
    1717	the existing 35+ pseudorandomness measures (all of which are local /
    1718	1-dimensional). If matched, primes are BGK-saturating in the strongest
    1719	combinatorial sense.
    1720	
    1721	**Why frontier:** sum-product is THE canonical test of "no joint
    1722	structure under both addition and multiplication." Primes ARE the
    1723	multiplicative atoms but their additive structure is constrained only
